                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Eets (2006) stands as a historic milestone marking the debut of the legendary indie studio Klei Entertainment, predating their creation of masterpieces like Don't Starve and Mark of the Ninja. Available on Steam, this 2D puzzle-strategy game features charming, hand-drawn visuals reminiscent of Flash cartoons. Rather than relying on direct action controls, the game offers a logic-based experience that is both relaxed and challenging, requiring players to guide a cute white creature named Eets through obstacle-filled labyrinths.\n\nThe game's main appeal lies in its unique character-emotion mechanics and a blend of gameplay styles akin to Lemmings and The Incredible Machine. Players do not manually control Eets' movement; instead, they manipulate the creature's mood and behavior using food or environmental objects. Facial indicators reveal Eets' state: an angry Eets jumps further, while a frightened one refuses to walk near the edge of a precipice. The experience is further enhanced by dozens of absurd tools—ranging from exploding flying pigs and chocolate-chip clouds to a mischievous whale that can launch Eets across the map instantly.\n\nHowever, as a 2006 debut project, the game suffers from a steep difficulty curve and repetitive elements in certain areas. Some late-game levels demand precise object placement and tedious trial-and-error, which can frustrate casual players. Although it previously faced compatibility issues on modern PCs—leading to its temporary removal—recent Steam updates have made it free to play and fully functional once more. This classic title is highly recommended as a digital historical artifact for retro puzzle enthusiasts eager to witness the early creative roots of Klei Entertainment.",
